WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.060
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:04.820
we take a look at the read/write
performance of our storage

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:05.479
buckets.

00:00:05.479 --> 00:00:07.520
Will everything fit in
the overhead compartments?

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:08.540
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:10.994 --> 00:00:12.410
Over the last few
weeks, we've had

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:14.368
a lot of interest from
web developers regarding

00:00:14.368 --> 00:00:16.580
the performance of Google
Cloud Storage, which

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:19.940
is listed as a unified object
storage solution in the Cloud.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.560
Or in simpler
terms, it's a place

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.400
in the cloud to serve files.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.120
We've got some followup articles
on exact questions they ask,

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:27.619
but before we get
to those, I wanted

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:29.270
to establish a
baseline of performance

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:31.580
for reading and writing to GCS.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.710
For most web developers,
read performance from GCS

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:34.987
is kind of a big deal.

00:00:34.987 --> 00:00:36.820
I mean, any time you
use it for static data,

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:40.880
like webs or game files, you
want that as fast as possible.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.400
To test this, we placed an
asset in a region bucket

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.770
in US Central, and
then fetched that asset

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:48.150
from a bunch of machines
located around the world.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.540
Now, because this
bucket is regional,

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:52.820
the graph shows that the
farther away the request,

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:56.150
the higher the latency due
to-- you know-- physics.

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:58.790
Turning on caching or using
a multi-regional bucket

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:00.904
should easily fix
this, but we'll

00:01:00.904 --> 00:01:02.445
talk about that in
a different video.

00:01:02.445 --> 00:01:03.770
Just stay tuned.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.500
Of course, on the other side of
the coin is write performance.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.320
If you've got a lot of users
uploading images from their 3G

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.860
devices straight into GCS, then
write performance is probably

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:14.810
a big deal for you.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:17.180
To test this, let's just
upload the same file

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.100
under different names
to the US Central

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.490
1 bucket from various
places in the world.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:24.050
Now, again, the graph
shows that the farther away

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:26.280
uploads take a
little longer time,

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.720
but it's within expectations
given the size of the file.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.480
Now, direct read/write
performance is easy to test,

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.197
but in honesty, there's
lots of variables

00:01:34.197 --> 00:01:35.780
here that influence
performance, which

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.930
make it a pain to write
proper benchmarking for.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:41.180
Thankfully, you don't have
to write all of those tests

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:41.900
yourself.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:46.310
The gsutil perfdiag command
runs a suite of diagnostic tests

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:48.260
for a given cloud
storage bucket,

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.630
giving information on
latency and throughput

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:54.440
tests for read, write, delete,
download, metadata, and upload

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:55.710
operations.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.230
So if you're ever
in doubt about how

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:01.130
your GCS bucket is performing,
perfdiag is a great first step.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.860
And if you're in doubt about the
rest of your Cloud performance,

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.260
check out the associated
article for this video linked

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:07.490
in the description below.

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:09.614
And don't forget to check
out the rest of our Cloud

00:02:09.614 --> 00:02:11.210
Performance Atlas
content because when

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:13.550
it comes to performance,
every millisecond counts.

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:16.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

